I am very careful about spelling errors, and I *always* proofread my own
messages.  (Hence, my small joke about the OP's (minor and totally
understandable) typo.)  Regarding punctuation, I try to follow standard
English conventions (insofar as there *are* such things) when I feel
that it affects (however slightly) the semantic content of the message. 
My ellipses are a bit of shorthand, which I use mainly to emulate my
speech patterns.  As for capitalization... well, I plead nolo contendre
on that one.  Capitalization "errors" rarely bother *me*, and the
English standards are pretty arbitrary.  (E.g., who decided "I" should
always be capitalized, but not "You"?) 

I've noticed that I use two distinct styles of posting.  When I post
things like YANIs or thoughtful discussions, I generally (but not
always) use correct grammar, punctuation, etc.  But when I'm just
jotting down a quick frivolous or facetious note, I tend to write the
way I usually speak: informally. 

However, for others' ease of understanding, I'll try to use "correct"
English in my posts from now on.  I honestly didn't realize that it
bothered some folks. 

But if I slip into old patterns from time to time, I hope I will be
forgiven, as I forgive those who trespass against me. ;)  (Spelling and
gross grammatical errors are big pet peeves of mine as well, actually.) 

But no one can make me capitalize my own name, dammit! :)

